---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Hero from '../../components/Hero.astro';
import CTABlock from '../../components/CTABlock.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
	title="How to Choose an Excavation Contractor | D&D Land Works"
	description="What to check before hiring an excavation contractor in Oregon: CCB license, bonding, DEQ certification for septic, and questions worth asking upfront."
>
	<Hero
		eyebrow="Hiring Guide"
		headline="How to Choose an Excavation Contractor"
		subheadline="Anyone can own a skid steer. Not everyone should be trusted with your foundation, your drainage, or your septic system."
		primaryCta={{ label: 'Call 541-401-8726', href: 'tel:5414018726' }}
		secondaryCta={{ label: 'Get an Estimate', href: '/contact' }}
	/>
	<section class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 py-16">
		<h2 class="font-display font-bold uppercase tracking-tight text-3xl text-ink">
			Check the License First
		</h2>
		<p class="mt-4 text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
			Oregon requires excavation contractors to carry a CCB license through the Construction
			Contractors Board, and that number is public. You can look it up on the CCB's website before a
			contractor ever sets foot on your property, and you should. A license also means the contractor
			carries the bonding and insurance the state requires, so you're not the one left covering
			damage if something goes wrong.
		</p>
		<h2 class="mt-12 font-display font-bold uppercase tracking-tight text-3xl text-ink">
			Septic Work Needs a Separate Certification
		</h2>
		<p class="mt-4 text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
			A general excavation license doesn't cover septic install or repair. Oregon's DEQ requires its
			own certification for that, and a contractor without it legally can't do the work, no matter
			how capable their crew looks with an excavator. If septic is part of the job, ask specifically
			whether the contractor holds DEQ certification, not just a CCB number.
		</p>
		<h2 class="mt-12 font-display font-bold uppercase tracking-tight text-3xl text-ink">
			Questions Worth Asking Before You Hire
		</h2>
		<ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-slate-700 list-disc list-inside">
			<li>What's your CCB license number, and how long have you held it?</li>
			<li>Are you DEQ certified, if the job involves septic work?</li>
			<li>Who actually runs the equipment on my job, you or a sub?</li>
			<li>Will you walk the site before quoting, or price it off a description?</li>
			<li>What happens if you hit rock, poor soil, or something unexpected mid-job?</li>
		</ul>
		<p class="mt-4 text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
			A contractor who hesitates on the license and certification questions is worth walking away
			from. A contractor who wants to see the site before committing to a number usually isn't
			hiding anything, they're just being accurate.
		</p>
		<h2 class="mt-12 font-display font-bold uppercase tracking-tight text-3xl text-ink">
			Get Multiple Estimates, Compare the Same Scope
		</h2>
		<p class="mt-4 text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
			Two bids that look far apart sometimes aren't quoting the same work. Make sure each estimate
			covers the same scope, clearing, grading, drainage, compaction, before comparing the bottom
			line. D&D Land Works is licensed and bonded under CCB #261742 and DEQ certified for septic,
			and quotes are always based on a site visit, not a phone description.
		</p>
	</section>
	<CTABlock
		headline="Verify, Then Call"
		subheadline="Check the license, then get a free on-site estimate."
	/>
</BaseLayout>
